  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Consider the quadratic function $y=ax^2+bx+c$ where $a,b,c\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The graph of this function passes through the points $(-2,0)$ and $(4,0)$, and $y$ has a maximum value of $54$. Find $(a,b,c)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,b,c)=(-6,12,48)$.\n\nThe vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at $(1,54)$; substitute it into intercept form.",
      "worked": "The intercepts give $y=a(x+2)(x-4)$. The maximum occurs halfway between them:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-2+4}{2}\\\\x&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nAt the vertex, $y=54$:\n$$\\begin{align*}54&=a(1+2)(1-4)\\\\54&=-9a\\\\a&=-6.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=-6(x+2)(x-4)\\\\y&=-6(x^2-2x-8)\\\\y&=-6x^2+12x+48.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $(a,b,c)=(-6,12,48)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
